Other Good Patterns

Game Data Context — Give every handler access to a shared game_data dict (or a GameState object) for flags, counters, plot state.
Dynamic Loading — Use importlib as shown (clean and Pythonic). Register adventures in a central adventures/__init__.py if needed.
Base Adventure Class (optional, for more structure):Python

class Adventure:
    def get_handlers(self) -> Dict[str, Callable]: ...
    def get_data(self): ...  # rooms, etc.
    
Then each adventure implements a subclass or provides a factory function.
Command Registration — Allow adventures to add entirely new verbs via a custom_commands dict in handlers.
Testing — Each adventure can have its own tests/ with mocks of the engine.

This mirrors the successful event handler system used in the official Eamon Remastered web port (which separates core logic from per-adventure JS handlers).
Benefits

Core engine stays stable and bug-free across all adventures.
Adventures are truly standalone (just drop in a new folder with JSON data + handlers.py).
Easy to extend: add new hook points in engine.py as needed (e.g., on_monster_death, on_enter_room, before_combat, end_turn, etc.).
Scalable for future features.

